  "outputSample": "In local inference benchmarks, reporting only a single aggregate metric (like \"tokens per second\") is insufficient because Large Language Model (LLM) inference consists of two fundamentally different computational phases with distinct bottlenecks. To accurately assess performance, user experience, and hardware suitability, you must report **Prompt Prefill Throughput**, **Decode Throughput**, and **Time to First Token (TTFT)**.\n\nHere’s why each metric is critical:\n\n### 1. Prompt Prefill Throughput (Processing Input)\n**What it measures:** The speed at which the model processes the input prompt (the \"prefill\" phase). This is a **compute-bound** operation where the model processes all input tokens in parallel.\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- **Long Context Performance:** For applications with large context windows (e.g., RAG, document analysis, codebases), prefill dominates the total latency. A model might have fast decode speeds but crawl when processing a 100k-token prompt.\n- **Hardware Utilization:** Prefill heavily utilizes GPU compute cores (FLOPs). It reveals how well your GPU handles matrix multiplications and whether your model quantization or kernel optimizations are effective for large inputs.\n- **Batching Efficiency:** Prefill",
